    <h2 class=3D"fw-bold fs-700" id=3D"syllabus">CPE Exam Syllabus</h2>
    <hr>
   =20
    <h2 class=3D"fw-bold fs-650">Exam Overview</h2>
   =20
<p>The CPE certification exam assesses foundational skills in C++ programmi=
ng. It verifies the candidate=E2=80=99s ability to write simple programs, a=
pply basic programming constructs, understand fundamental concepts, and use=
 standard library elements. The exam focuses on syntax, program logic, data=
 manipulation, functions, pointers, structures, and strings.</p>

<p>The CPE exam consists of 30 questions (items), each carrying a different=
 score value based on its complexity and objective. The maximum total score=
 is 120 points, which is then normalized and converted into a percentage. T=
o pass, a candidate must achieve a cumulative score of 70% or higher across=
 all questions =E2=80=93 the result is based on the total points earned, no=
t on a simple average of scores per exam block.</p>

<p>The scoring system is based on psychometric analysis, where each questio=
n is assigned a weight and point value reflecting its difficulty and import=
ance. This ensures that the exam measures knowledge and skills fairly, with=
 the final score representing the total points earned across all questions,=
 rather than a simple count of correct answers.</p>

   =20
<h2 class=3D"fw-bold fs-650">Exam Objectives by Block</h2>

<p><span style=3D"font-weight: bold;">Block 1 =E2=80=93 Syntax, Literals, a=
nd Operators</span>
<br>(Exam items: 9 | Weight: 28%)</p>

<ol style=3D"list-style-type: none; padding-left: 10px;">
    <li>1.1 Identify valid C++ syntax elements, keywords, and code structur=
es.</li>
    <li>1.2 Recognize C++ built-in data types and corresponding literals.</=
li>
    <li>1.3 Describe the structure and declaration of the <span style=3D"fo=
nt-style: italic;">main()</span> function and its parameters.</li>
    <li>1.4 Apply arithmetic, relational, logical, bitwise, and assignment =
operators.</li>
    <li>1.5 Determine operator precedence, associativity, and the impact on=
 expressions.</li>
    <li>1.6 Apply short-circuit logic in control expressions.</li>
    <li>1.7 Use basic input/output streams (<span style=3D"font-style: ital=
ic;">cin</span>, <span style=3D"font-style: italic;">cout</span>, <span sty=
le=3D"font-style: italic;">cerr</span>) and manipulators (<span style=3D"fo=
nt-style: italic;">endl</span>, <span style=3D"font-style: italic;">setw</s=
pan>).</li>
</ol>
<br>

<p><span style=3D"font-weight: bold;">Block 2 =E2=80=93 Flow Control and Fu=
nctions</span>
<br>(Exam items: 8 | Weight: 28%)</p>

<ol style=3D"list-style-type: none; padding-left: 10px;">
    <li>2.1 Describe and use conditional statements (<span style=3D"font-st=
yle: italic;">if</span>, <span style=3D"font-style: italic;">else</span>).<=
/li>
    <li>2.2 Use loop constructs (<span style=3D"font-style: italic;">while<=
/span>, <span style=3D"font-style: italic;">do</span>, <span style=3D"font-=
style: italic;">for</span>) and loop control statements (<span style=3D"fon=
t-style: italic;">break</span>, <span style=3D"font-style: italic;">continu=
e</span>).</li>
    <li>2.3 Recognize the purpose of the <span style=3D"font-style: italic;=
">goto</span> statement and labeled statements.</li>
    <li>2.4 Apply multiple-selection structures using <span style=3D"font-s=
tyle: italic;">switch</span>, <span style=3D"font-style: italic;">case</spa=
n>, and <span style=3D"font-style: italic;">default</span> keywords.</li>
    <li>2.5 Define, declare, and invoke functions with correct syntax.</li>
    <li>2.6 Use <span style=3D"font-style: italic;">return</span> statement=
s in typed and <span style=3D"font-style: italic;">void</span> functions.</=
li>
    <li>2.7 Pass arguments by value, by reference, and by pointer.</li>
    <li>2.8 Apply basic recursion to solve simple problems.</li>
</ol>
<br>

<p><span style=3D"font-weight: bold;">Block 3 =E2=80=93 Vectors and Pointer=
s</span>
<br>(Exam items: 7 | Weight: 24%)</p>

<ol style=3D"list-style-type: none; padding-left: 10px;">
    <li>3.1 Declare, initialize, and manipulate vectors and arrays, includi=
ng multidimensional arrays.</li>
    <li>3.2 Access vector data using the <span style=3D"font-style: italic;=
">data()</span> method.</li>
    <li>3.3 Declare and initialize pointers, including the use of <span sty=
le=3D"font-style: italic;">nullptr</span>.</li>
    <li>3.4 Dereference pointers and use the address-of operator (<span sty=
le=3D"font-style: italic;">&amp;</span>).</li>
    <li>3.5 Perform pointer conversions using <span style=3D"font-style: it=
alic;">static_cast</span> and <span style=3D"font-style: italic;">dynamic_c=
ast</span>.</li>
    <li>3.6 Manage dynamic memory with <span style=3D"font-style: italic;">=
new</span>, <span style=3D"font-style: italic;">delete</span>, and <span st=
yle=3D"font-style: italic;">delete[]</span>.</li>
</ol>
<br>

<p><span style=3D"font-weight: bold;">Block 4 =E2=80=93 Structures and Stri=
ngs</span>
<br>(Exam items: 6 | Weight: 20%)</p>

<ol style=3D"list-style-type: none; padding-left: 10px;">
    <li>4.1 Declare and define structures.</li>
    <li>4.2 Access structure members using the dot (<span style=3D"font-sty=
le: italic;">.</span>) operator.</li>
    <li>4.3 Declare vectors of structures and access their fields.</li>
    <li>4.4 Declare, initialize, and manipulate <span style=3D"font-style: =
italic;">std::string</span> objects.</li>
    <li>4.5 Perform basic string operations and comparisons.</li>
</ol>
<br>
   =20
    <h2 class=3D"fw-bold fs-650">MQC Profile</h2>

<p>A Minimally Qualified Candidate (MQC) for the <span style=3D"font-style:=
 italic;">CPE =E2=80=93 C++ Certified Entry-Level Programmer</span> certifi=
cation understands the fundamental concepts of C++ programming and can appl=
y them to write simple, correct programs that demonstrate practical use of =
core language features.</p>

<p>The candidate demonstrates knowledge of C++ syntax, data types, operator=
s, and input/output mechanisms (Block 1), and is able to control program fl=
ow using conditional statements, loops, and functions, including basic recu=
rsion and parameter passing (Block 2). They can declare, initialize, and ma=
nipulate vectors, arrays, and pointers, and manage dynamic memory safely (B=
lock 3). Additionally, the candidate is capable of defining and using struc=
tures and strings, performing basic operations, and organizing simple data =
within a program (Block 4).</p>

<p>The MQC is prepared to advance to higher-level certifications and apply =
entry-level C++ skills in practical programming tasks.</p>
